WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.689 --> 00:00:19.630
WOLTERSTORFF: Good to be back. Good to

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.269
be back at Yale and teach-

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:25.309
ing this course once again on this really
important topic.

00:00:25.309 --> 00:00:27.140
And

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.930
I'll assume that nothing much has

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:32.480
changed in the ethos of Yale students
in the ten years since I re-- not quite ten

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.710
years since I retired, so that you're still feisty
and you have...

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.750
disagreements.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.590
So the description for today's seminar that
I was sent

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.490
opposes two quite different though related
questions.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:46.900
One is this:

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.790
Do religious people have good reasons to
support pluralistic democracy?

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:54.760
Or to quote the precise words of the description,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.559
"How can we encourage adherents of exclusive
faith traditions

00:00:58.559 --> 00:01:04.580
to affirm the political project of liberal--
pluralistic democracy?"

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.280
And the other question that I--

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.450
that was in the

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:08.790
description I got

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.000
was this:

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.120
What is the role of religious reasons in public
political debate

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.420
within a pluralistic democracy?

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.040
Or in the precise words of the description:

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.630
"For what reasons and under what conditions

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.059
should democratic citizens appeal to matters
of religious faith

00:01:25.059 --> 00:01:27.939
in public discourse?"

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:29.040
I trust

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.659
you see, as I saw,

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:33.540
the difference between those two questions.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.600
The first asks whether religious people have
good reasons

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.440
to support a certain kind of political structure,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.720
namely pluralist, liberal

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.770
democracy.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:48.380
The second assumes the existence of a
religiously

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.820
pluralistic democracy,

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:53.800
and it asks what should be the role of religious
reasons within

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.120
such a democracy

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.740
in public debate about

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.090
political issues.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.239
Both of those--

00:02:00.239 --> 00:02:05.540
support for the structure, and reasons were
political debate within the structure--

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.190
are important.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.739
Both are huge.

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:14.760
The sensible thing for me would be to choose
one of the two and talk only about it, but

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.579
I've chosen today not to be sensible,

00:02:17.579 --> 00:02:21.199
and to say something about each of them.

00:02:21.199 --> 00:02:22.829
In the order.

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:23.769
Of mention.

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:28.359
What I say on the first is going to overlap
interestingly with some things Miroslav said, and

00:02:28.359 --> 00:02:31.989
we did not coordinate this.

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:34.099
So start with the first:

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.399
Little bit of really quick

00:02:36.399 --> 00:02:38.459
brisk history.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:44.099
Liberal or pluralistic democracy emerged in
the West out of the breakdown of Christendom.

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:46.229
And what was Christendom?

00:02:46.229 --> 00:02:50.859
Well, Christendom didn't assume a religiously unified
society.

00:02:50.859 --> 00:02:54.349
Specifically, it assumed a society in which
everybody was Christian, in which everybody

00:02:54.349 --> 00:02:56.819
was baptized.

00:02:56.819 --> 00:02:57.840
Of course

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.209
(parenthesis)

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:00.529
everybody knew

00:03:00.529 --> 00:03:04.139
that here in there with in Europe there were
people who were not baptized--

00:03:04.139 --> 00:03:06.359
in particular Jews. Not only, but

00:03:06.359 --> 00:03:08.229
in particular.

00:03:08.229 --> 00:03:09.779
But the theory did not take 

00:03:09.779 --> 00:03:11.659
account of their presence,

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:13.529
with the result, as you know,

00:03:13.529 --> 00:03:19.249
that the position of Jews was not only theoretically
anomalous, they were often politically precarious.

00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:20.909
They had no

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:22.749
clear position in the structure

00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:26.259
unlike the Millets in Islam.

00:03:26.259 --> 00:03:29.980
Now, given a society of Christians, the basic
idea of Christendom

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:33.059
was this: that this

00:03:33.059 --> 00:03:38.829
religiously unified society-- that's a crucial
part of it-- that this religiously unified society

00:03:38.829 --> 00:03:40.189
had two rules

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:43.569
as they were standardly called-- two rules, two

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:45.589
governances:

00:03:45.589 --> 00:03:46.950
both being

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:47.870
manifestations of

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.759
God's rule.

00:03:49.759 --> 00:03:52.299
One rule was that of the church

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:56.029
and one rule was that of the empire.

00:03:56.029 --> 00:03:59.409
And since both were over the same people,

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:04.209
the differentiation between these two roles
could not be by reference to the subjects,

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:06.840
but had to be by reference to the aspects
of

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:07.849
peoples'

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:09.959
lives that these two rules

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:11.459
governed, right? So

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:12.939
one people but

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:15.930
two different rules over different aspects of their lives.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.279
So it was a division of powers

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.419
rather than a division of

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:22.900
subjects.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.949
Often it was said that the church ruled over
the spiritual life of the people

00:04:26.949 --> 00:04:32.179
and that the empire ruled over the secular
life of the people.

00:04:32.179 --> 00:04:33.410
The latter--

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.190
that the empire rules over the secular life--

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:39.060
was by no means interpreted to mean

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.319
that the empire stayed out of moral and religious
matters.

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:47.770
It was on the concrete--to the best of my knowledge--
universally agreed

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.650
that it was the duty of the emperor-- the ruler--

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.770
to cultivate morality and true religion
in the people.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:58.869
And not just to cultivate.

00:04:58.869 --> 00:05:02.589
To enforce.

00:05:02.589 --> 00:05:04.280
Now, as you would guess,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.930
there were all kinds of hassles

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:09.490
over what sect-- over what the spiritual-
secular division

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.079
came from, so

00:05:11.079 --> 00:05:15.159
Western Christendom was filled with hassles
on that score and-- and by and large they were

00:05:15.159 --> 00:05:17.050
settled less by

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.820
theoretical acuity than by who had the appropriate

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.240
power at the moment. 

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.719
So you got the picture?

00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:28.210
A religiously unified people

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:31.889
under these two rules.

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:35.709
And I think it was pretty clearly the break-up of
the Western church--

00:05:35.709 --> 00:05:37.320
oh the Eastern,

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.689
Eastern Europe,

00:05:38.689 --> 00:05:42.210
I'd have to say different things about it, sorry.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.759
So it was the break-up of the Western church
in the fifteen hundreds that eventually caused

00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:50.340
the collapse of Christendom and forced Europe

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.639
to rethink 

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:54.600
its political structure and the relation between
that

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.919
and religion.

00:05:56.919 --> 00:05:58.899
At first it experimented with

00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:00.710
little Christendoms,

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.859
many Christendoms.

00:06:02.859 --> 00:06:05.530
Germany would be a Lutheran Christendom,

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.319
Spain would continue to be a Catholic Christendom,

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:11.360
Switzerland would be a reform

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.890
Christendom, Scotland would be a Presbyterian

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:15.089
Christendom, and

00:06:15.089 --> 00:06:17.719
England would be an Anglican Christendom,

00:06:17.719 --> 00:06:21.449
and so forth.

00:06:21.449 --> 00:06:22.710
But. Very soon

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.599
religious diversity got inside of all these

00:06:25.599 --> 00:06:28.699
many Christendoms,

00:06:28.699 --> 00:06:31.669
and that idea of Christendom became impossible;

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:34.899
both maxi and mini forms became

00:06:34.899 --> 00:06:36.519
impossible as a--

00:06:36.519 --> 00:06:39.610
political structure.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.419
Well, to take enormous leaps:

00:06:42.419 --> 00:06:46.629
after centuries of confusion, ferment, and bloodshed,

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:48.370
there eventually emerged,

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:52.089
towards the end of the seventeen hundreds
the American experiment

00:06:52.089 --> 00:06:53.560
of liberal

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:53.810
pluralistic

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:55.089
democracy.

00:06:55.089 --> 00:06:57.709
There was a lot of stuff in between, 

00:06:57.709 --> 00:07:00.800
so it's not just out of the blue.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:01.749
It was--

00:07:01.749 --> 00:07:03.030
I think we tend to forget--

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:07.720
a radical and a risky experiment.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:11.180
Much about it had never been tried before,

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.099
and in particular,

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:16.110
nothing like the relation between religion
and the state,

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.770
that the constitutional founders proposed,

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:23.489
had ever been tried before.

00:07:23.489 --> 00:07:24.759
So here is

00:07:24.759 --> 00:07:27.489
the central question:

00:07:27.489 --> 00:07:32.049
And what was the new proposed relation between
the religions of the people

00:07:32.049 --> 00:07:32.850
and the state,

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.509
the American state?

00:07:35.509 --> 00:07:40.140
I think we can probably summarize it in the
following four structural

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:44.560
principles.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.410
First,

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.300
church and state are to be distinct

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:51.909
institutions.

00:07:51.909 --> 00:07:54.079
Synagogue and the state,

00:07:54.079 --> 00:07:56.939
mosque and the state. You get the point.

00:07:56.939 --> 00:07:58.659
There's to be a distinction, a clear distinction between

00:07:58.659 --> 00:08:04.329
ecclesiastical religious institutions and the state.

00:08:04.329 --> 00:08:05.789
Of course,

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:07.049
as I've just observed,

00:08:07.049 --> 00:08:11.029
there had long been such a distinction in the West. 

00:08:11.029 --> 00:08:12.569
So this was not an

00:08:12.569 --> 00:08:15.259
innovation on the part of the American founders;
they were

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:16.430
carrying on

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:22.080
what they knew from their Western antecedents
of clear institutional distinctions

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.659
between church and state. Second--

00:08:24.659 --> 00:08:27.770
that was the first--

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:31.319
the second important structural principle was

00:08:31.319 --> 00:08:34.050
freedom of religious exercise.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:36.260
The state is to 

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.840
not govern

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:38.910
how people

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:43.210
exercise their religion.

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:45.410
Third,

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:47.440
the state

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.560
when distributing benefits and burdens

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:55.420
is not to differentiate in its treatment
of citizens

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:58.420
on account of their religion or lack

00:08:58.420 --> 00:08:59.540
thereof. I'm going to say it again:

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:04.270
the state, in its distribution of benefits
and burdens,

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.029
is not to discriminate

00:09:06.029 --> 00:09:07.630
among its citizens

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.560
on the basis of their religion

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.050
or lack thereof.

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.180
It is going to discriminate on some other bases

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:14.569
but it's not to discriminate

00:09:14.569 --> 00:09:17.950
on that basis.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.210
And here's a fourth principle. 

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:27.290
Fourth, there is to be no differentiation among citizens

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:32.540
in their right to hold office in the state

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:33.150
and

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.590
in their right to--

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:37.760
to a political voice

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.070
in the conduct of the personnel of the state.

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:44.930
No discrimination among people on the basis
of their religion as to whether you can hold

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.260
office,

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:50.380
or whether you've got voice in 

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:50.950
electing

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:53.650
people for

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:57.600
positions in the state.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.610
What do you think? I think those four principles,
structural principles about 

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.940
the relation of religion and the state,

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:06.090
are essential to the very idea

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.640
of a liberal

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.900
pluralistic democracy.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.360
You can have a

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.800
democracy in the absence of those four principles,

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.760
in the sense that you can have people voting

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:21.880
for positions in the

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.060
regime and so forth,

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:25.610
but you don't have a

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:26.560
liberal

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:27.079
pluralistic

00:10:27.079 --> 00:10:31.910
democracy.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.390
Okay.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:35.050
Let's not--

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:37.520
and this gets to Miroslav's point--

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.130
let's not conceal from ourselves the fact

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:43.660
that acceptance of those four principles

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:48.200
requires a lot of-- I'll call it this-- requires a lot
of shaping up

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:50.540
on the part of both the state

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:51.990
and the religions

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:55.300
present in society.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:56.270
Each party,

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:57.770
the state

00:10:57.770 --> 00:10:59.670
and the religions,

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:05.570
have to refrain from doing a lot of what they
have historically done.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:09.070
Now fortunately, at the time of the American
founding,

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.150
most of this shaping up

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:16.020
had already occurred or was about to occur,
in the US with respect to the state

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.660
and the religions. People were beginning to
think

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.010
along these lines, or

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:21.490
nobody had to

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:25.340
force them into thinking along these lines.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:26.310
But,

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:27.460
to repeat,

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:29.100
it was historically

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.669
a radical and risky

00:11:31.669 --> 00:11:33.520
experiment.

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:38.570
A state with no official religion,

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:43.200
a state with no official religion

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:50.050
in any way had never seriously been tried
before.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.010
Now we're all aware of the fact

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:54.780
that many religious people around the globe

00:11:54.780 --> 00:12:00.940
do not like this kind of democracy.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:02.040
And I dare say that

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:05.040
many of you, probably most of you, are aware

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:06.280
that some of those people

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:08.000
are located in this country--

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:10.450
that is, in the United States.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:11.080
The US is not

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:14.360
"this country" for all of you here, I'm sure.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:17.090
That is to say-- let me put it more bluntly--

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:21.910
there are a lot of present-day American Christians
who do not like liberal democracy,

00:12:21.910 --> 00:12:24.790
that phase of it I've described.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:26.460
That was clear from

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:29.020
the Howard West quotation. 

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:33.240
A good many Christian leaders, including some
theologians, have what I can only think

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.030
of as an extremely sour

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:41.660
attitude towards liberal democracy.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:45.060
Now, a number of reasons for that, as you'd expect,

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:49.810
but if I hear correctly, there is one common
theme running through those in this country

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:51.670
and abroad.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:54.410
And that thing is this:

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:55.709
once upon a time

00:12:55.709 --> 00:12:59.440
the state was the highest expression,

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.990
or one of the two highest expressions,

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:07.980
of the religion-moral unity of a people.

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:08.870
Here's

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.100
where their

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:17.100
religious moral unity came to its topmost
institutional expression.

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:18.880
A liberal

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:20.690
democratic society is committed to its not

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:25.920
being that.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:29.830
That is, it's not giving expression to one particular
religious party.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:34.380
It's committed to being no more than an arrangement
of convenience, so it's said, 

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:39.170
whereby a number of such distinct communities can live together.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:40.890
But it itself

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:42.250
is a-

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:44.290
religious.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:47.210
It's a-moral.

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:52.090
An arrangement of convenience for such communities.

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:53.220
And--

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:54.900
okay, here's the real point--

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.860
and

00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:03.270
thereby it forces a dichotomy into the
lives of religious people.

00:14:03.270 --> 00:14:08.610
It forces them to divorce their religion from
their politics.

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:12.550
The Muslims who live in this country cannot live by sharia;

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:15.130
they can't.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:19.950
And you can fill out the counterpart for any other religion
you want.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.770
One of the most influential twentieth-century Muslim writers-- I'm going to mispronounce his

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.329
name, I'm sure, so you can tell--

00:14:25.329 --> 00:14:30.270
those of you who know how to pronounce it
will tell me afterwards-- Sayyid Qutb--

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:35.230
an Egyptian intellectual who was executed by the Egyptian government in

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:37.320
nineteen sixty-six.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:41.010
Qutb had lived for a time in the US, out
in Colorado,

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:45.050
and had found American life abhorrent.

00:14:45.050 --> 00:14:47.970
In the lives of America there is what he called-

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:48.649
marvelous phrase--

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:51.760
"a hideous schizophrenia."

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.480
A hideous schizophrenia

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.830
between religion and life.

00:14:57.830 --> 00:15:00.990
And he thought this "hideous schizophrenia,"

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.940
had its origins in original Christianity,

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:07.980
where he thought that Jesus had spiritualized the

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.350
Jewish religion.

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:11.820
And he thought that

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:13.250
liberal democracy,

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:15.490
by its very structure,

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:17.250
expressed

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:18.000
and reinforced

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.190
this hideous

00:15:19.190 --> 00:15:20.840
schizophrenia.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:26.200
It forced the privatization of religion.

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.470
And if the four principles that I cited

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:34.220
are indeed basic structural principles of
liberal democracy,

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:35.700
then

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:38.430
in a structural sort of way,

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.780
he's right about that.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:42.790
That kind of shaping up

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.850
of religions

00:15:44.850 --> 00:15:45.540
required

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:49.060
by the very structure of the liberal democracy.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:53.100
So pull it together like this: if religious people
have good reasons

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:55.130
for supporting

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:58.670
liberal pluralistic democracy,

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:03.230
those reasons will have to support those four
principles and the

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:05.570
kind of division

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:08.860
that I mentioned.

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:10.790
Now, I think that some religious people

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:11.639
do have

00:16:11.639 --> 00:16:16.060
good reasons for supporting those four principles.

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:20.020
To speak for myself, I think Christianity provides such reasons, though

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:24.660
there are a lot of my fellow Christians
who disagree.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:29.220
Now, I said I was going to talk about both
questions, but I've really almost eaten up

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:33.410
my time. So what do you think, Miroslav? Should I just
cut off what I have to say about

00:16:33.410 --> 00:16:35.570
religious reasons in

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:36.519
liberal democracy?

00:16:36.519 --> 00:16:40.090
VOLF [off-camera]: No.

00:16:40.090 --> 00:16:40.950
[Laughter.]

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:46.750
VOLF [off-camera]: Otherwise it would remain a secret.

00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:51.330
Okay, so now the second question. So my citation
of those four principles may well have left

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:52.780
you thinking,

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:53.919
as it did Qutb,

00:16:53.919 --> 00:16:58.600
that there just isn't any place for religious
reasons in public debate in a

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:00.530
democracy.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:02.690
You may have concluded that the

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:06.690
principles, those four principles rule it out.

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:11.140
And quite a few writers in recent years
have made that inference-- that the very idea

00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:16.700
of a liberal democracy rules out the use of
religious reasons in public debate.

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:19.780
But I think that's a mistake.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:21.910
But government in the ways I mentioned

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:27.870
has to be impartial in its treatment of citizens
with respect to the religion or their lack thereof.

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:30.570
I think that does not imply

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:31.340
excluding

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:32.240
religious reasons,

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:35.210
it excludes thinking religiously about

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:39.590
political topics, and speaking of them.

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:41.510
Now in your reading for today

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:45.500
there are some pieces that introduce you to
that debate that's been going on for two decades--

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:47.890
whether--

00:17:47.890 --> 00:17:50.370
so you know the basic issues.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:51.910
I'm not going to

00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:56.200
dabble into all of them, but I think it may be
helpful to bring these two topics together

00:17:56.200 --> 00:18:03.040
if I briefly review why John Rawls thought it was
one of the principles of liberal democracy

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.059
that citizens should use what he calls

00:18:06.059 --> 00:18:07.710
public reasons, and not

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:09.600
religious reasons, in public debate.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:13.890
So this will sort of tie the two together, okay? Rawls.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:15.030
I talked about

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:20.970
the structural features-- four of the structural
features of a liberal democracy.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.340
Rawls-- Rawls was of the view-- and

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:24.550
I share this view--

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:28.840
Rawls was of the view that behind these structural
principles,

00:18:28.840 --> 00:18:32.490
there's a certain cluster of ideas

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:36.320
about how citizens are to treat each other.

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:38.370
And that the structure

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.580
is an expression of these

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:41.620
background

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:42.520
implicit

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.720
ideas as to how we should treat each other.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.570
These background ideas make sense of the
structure.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:51.010
It's not just a

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:54.420
happenstance.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:56.210
And, Rawls thought then--

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:57.380
obviously--

00:18:57.380 --> 00:19:02.000
that citizens who really believe in liberal
democracy,

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:03.080
as opposed to just

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:05.510
putting up with it,

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:07.580
implicitly accept these

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:10.600
background explanatory principles.

00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:15.130
They don't have them at the top of their hands, it's implicit-- 
but they're implicitly

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:16.110
accepting it

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:18.430
if they agree with liberal democracy instead
of

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:21.640
just putting up with it.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:23.330
Here's one of those

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:25.320
important implicit

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.080
background principles for Rawls's thought:

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:34.670
"We are to treat each other as free and equal."

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:35.930
Fundamental

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:40.180
explanatory background principle to treat--
citizens are to treat each other as free and 

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:44.760
equal. Okay?

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:48.000
So now, says Rawls, ask yourself this question:

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.580
what does that principle--

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:52.170
treating each other as free and equal--

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:55.170
what does that principle imply 

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:56.610
for how we debate

00:19:56.610 --> 00:19:58.640
and decide political issues?

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.310
What does the free and equal principle imply

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:05.070
for how we conduct our debates?

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:07.809
And here's Rawls's answer:

00:20:07.809 --> 00:20:12.630
at least when it comes to important issues
of justice,

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:17.180
we should stand ready to appeal to principles

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:22.750
that we can expect all of our fellow citizens
to share.

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:24.700
Or at least all who

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.870
sincerely believe in liberal democracy-- the
others may not, sure--

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.890
we should appeal to principles that we can
expect

00:20:30.890 --> 00:20:36.860
all the believers-- our fellow believers in liberal
democracy-- to share.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:39.320
Otherwise-- here's Rawls's thought--

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:44.510
if we just go ahead and vote our own
view,

00:20:44.510 --> 00:20:47.870
we're imposing our views on the others and
not treating them

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:49.940
as equal.

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:52.220
I'm saying, "I know better than you

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:54.380
what the laws of this land should be like.

00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:55.749
I'm not treating you as

00:20:55.749 --> 00:21:00.490
a co-author of the laws."

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:04.360
It should be obvious at a glance that that
principle eliminates the use of religious

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:05.830
reasons,

00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:09.970
because you-- one thing you can be absolutely
sure of: any religious reason that you can

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:16.970
devise is is not going to be shared by some
of your present-day fellow Americans, right? Okay.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:22.290
So where do we find the shared reasons?

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:24.190
And Rawls's ingenious--

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:28.870
I think that it was a boot-strapping suggestion--
Rawls's ingenious suggestion was this:

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.020
here's where you can find the principles--

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:36.160
look back once again at those principles
implicit in liberal democracy

00:21:36.160 --> 00:21:39.610
that we got this first one from-- treating everybody
as free and equal.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:42.700
Look back at the other implicit principles.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:45.230
Everybody who accepts liberal democracy 

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:49.370
believes those, right? So that's going to be our--

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:52.060
body of our shared principles.

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:54.760
That's what he called public reason.

00:21:54.760 --> 00:21:56.230
It's going to take some work

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:59.550
to dig 'em out, they're implicit--

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:02.440
that's what political philosophers are for

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:03.640
and so forth--

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:06.460
but that's what we should appeal to.

00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:13.460
It won't matter, says Rawls, if people, if religious people add on some religious reasons.

00:22:13.919 --> 00:22:18.140
I don't want matter for debating minor matters.

00:22:19.340 --> 00:22:24.169
My memory of being in New Haven is that always
by Thanksgiving there is a great big

00:22:24.169 --> 00:22:27.539
tree-- Christmas tree on the Green, and there's
not this year, so New Haven must have

00:22:27.539 --> 00:22:28.430
had a

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:29.490
dispute about that.

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:33.350
And Rawls would say, "They don't have to avoid
using religious reasons to settle-- I mean it's 

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:35.340
unlike it-- well, somebody might--

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.620
you know, no big deal--

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:38.450
Huh?

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:44.750
Yeah, there, that's what I expected.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:47.480
So, let me talk to a conclusion.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:51.380
I'm not going to rehearse my reasons for thinking
that this proposal, ingenious and interesting

00:22:51.380 --> 00:22:53.350
as it is, doesn't work.

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:58.200
It's my impression that the state of the debate
over this now

00:22:58.200 --> 00:22:59.590
is that-- it's widely agreed--

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:04.049
that those who are opposed to using religious reasons
in public debate have not succeeded in making

00:23:04.049 --> 00:23:05.520
their case.

00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:06.190
So here's

00:23:06.190 --> 00:23:08.940
my own view, briefly presented.

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:15.000
With two crucial implications-- sorry, two crucial
qualifications--

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:15.880
my view is this:

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:20.240
I think it's okay for citizens to use whatever
reasons

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:24.110
that they themselves have for their political
views,

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:29.900
or whatever reasons they find cogent.

00:23:29.900 --> 00:23:35.180
Rarely if ever does it happen that consensus
emerges.

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:40.700
So I think a crucial part of liberal democracy
is a fair vote.

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:42.250
And I find it's

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:47.500
endlessly curious that Rawls and Audi and Lorimer and so forth-- Habermas--

00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:49.300
say almost nothing about vote.

00:23:49.300 --> 00:23:54.520
I think we almost never achieve consensus, so we
have to have a fair vote.

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:57.620
Here are the two qualifications:

00:23:57.620 --> 00:24:03.180
the entire debate takes place within the framework
of a constitution.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:07.140
Some proposals are just off the table,

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:09.980
or they're so hard to get onto the table

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:13.740
that they may just as well be off the table.

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:17.590
That's our great-- that's the great protection
in a liberal democracy against the rule--

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:20.140
oppressive rule of the majority.

00:24:20.140 --> 00:24:21.810
There's a constitution which says,

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:27.300
"These things can't be done!"

00:24:27.300 --> 00:24:28.690
Second, I've come to think that the

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:32.750
character of the debate is important-- how the
debate is conducted--

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:36.230
not so much its content, but how it's conducted.

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.820
Citizens are to treat each other civilly.

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:40.940
Become informed,

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:43.690
be willing to present their reasons to others,

00:24:43.690 --> 00:24:47.660
be willing to listen to the reasons of others.

00:24:47.660 --> 00:24:49.580
Let me just say parenthetically

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:50.870
that one of my great--

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:52.380
what word do I--

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:54.130
sorrows, laments, whatever,

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:59.490
is that American political dialogue is so un-civil, it is so chock full

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:03.350
of people demeaning their fellow citizens.

00:25:03.350 --> 00:25:05.070
Whatever be their reasons.

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:08.630
They should never demean their fellow citizens.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:10.820
So I think the debate over

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:14.429
whether religious reasons are acceptable
was an important debate to have,

00:25:14.429 --> 00:25:17.130
but I think the real discussion now has to be over

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:19.890
"And how should we conduct that debate so that we 

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:22.919
treat our fellow citizens with dignity

00:25:22.919 --> 00:25:23.830
and honor, and justice. And respect."

